rly that is something  that we need to be watching  but it's still only at around the kind  of one percent one point something  percent one point two percent or so  um that's something to keep an eye on  because the minute  you get up to kind of three percent five  percent that's something epidemiologists  are genuinely concerned about  but it's still relatively low so not a  flashing red light on this  this chart which is probably a better  way of measuring uh covert 19  in the country and not a flashing red  light as well when you kind of take that  line we showed you a moment ago this is  just uh  cases again this is a line of the bar  chart i showed you before going up quite  a bit recently  when you compare that to deaths and this  obviously the number of people dying of  diseases  is perhaps the most uh concerning of all  of the statistics that we've covered  over  the past few decades in this country  but have a look at the relationship  between these two lines so early on in  the pandemic  the number of deaths that black line was  rising in lockstep  with the number of cases but have a look  at what's happened more recently the  number of  cases has gone up but that black line  deaths has remained very low in fact the  number of deaths that we've seen  recently is about the lowest it's been  since the the onset of the disease kind  of around  10 maybe 20 a day but low double figures  numbers of deaths higher than anyone  would like but nonetheless lower than  they've been for quite some time  and it does look like there's been a  kind of the relationship between those  two lines has broken down  in a encouraging way now is this just  that we haven't yet seen you know is  there a lag  often you know people get covered 19 it  takes some time before they  face having to go to hospital and some  people sadly die so  are we just seeing a kind of lag and we  will see an impact from this in the next  uh few weeks well that's quite possible  but it so happens that i